what is networking?
Networking means connecting computers so they can communicate and exchange information.	

Understand five important concepts
Ip address - An address used to identify a device on a network. Example: 192.168.1.10.
Port - A number identifying a network service. For example, HTTP commonly uses port 80, HTTPS uses 443, and SSH uses 22.
DNS - Translates domain names such as google.com into IP addresses.
Client and server  - A client requests something; a server responds. Your browser is a client when it requests a webpage.
TCP and UDP - TCP provides reliable, ordered communication. UDP sends datagrams without the same delivery guarantees

ip addr - Check your IP addresses
ip link - his displays your network interfaces and their state.
ip route - A routing table helps Linux decide where to send network traffic.
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8 - Test connectivity
nslookup checks DNS resolution.

network ports:
SSH	        22
HTTP / Nginx	80
HTTPS	        443
MySQL	        3306
PostgreSQL	5432

Common HTTP status codes:
Code	Meaning
200	Success
301	Redirect
400	Bad request
401	Unauthorized
403	Forbidden
404	Not found
500	Internal server error
502	Bad gateway
503	Service unavailable

These codes are extremely useful when troubleshooting application deployments.

SSH means Secure Shell.

It allows you to securely connect to another Linux computer over a network.

